WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.890 --> 00:00:05.140
Every now and then, you come across a movie
that exists outside of the traditional ideas

00:00:05.140 --> 00:00:06.859
of “good” and “bad.”

00:00:06.859 --> 00:00:11.620
These are the weird ones: forgotten sequels,
offbeat ideas, and the obscure but secretly

00:00:11.620 --> 00:00:14.710
awesome cult classics that you just can’t
believe are real.

00:00:14.710 --> 00:00:20.660
Trust us, though: these movies actually exist,
and they're every bit as bizarre as they sound.

00:00:20.660 --> 00:00:21.929
Karate Bearfighter

00:00:21.929 --> 00:00:26.829
If movies were judged by the quality of their
titles, 1975's Karate Bearfighter would be

00:00:26.829 --> 00:00:29.369
considered one of the greatest films of all
time.

00:00:29.369 --> 00:00:34.000
Unfortunately, it’ll just have to settle
for being the greatest Man vs. Animal martial

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:36.910
arts spectacular that most people have never
heard of.

00:00:36.910 --> 00:00:42.420
The story follows the great Sonny Chiba as
Masutatsu Oyama, a karate master forced to

00:00:42.420 --> 00:00:46.539
fist-fight a bear to the death in order to
raise money to pay for a child’s medical

00:00:46.539 --> 00:00:47.539
bills.

00:00:47.539 --> 00:00:51.980
Admittedly, the actual bear fight isn't that
great and pretty clearly involves Chiba slugging

00:00:51.980 --> 00:00:55.940
it out with a man in an ill-fitting costume,
but it’s still fun to watch.

00:00:55.940 --> 00:00:56.940
And the best part?

00:00:56.940 --> 00:00:58.289
It's based on a true story.

00:00:58.289 --> 00:01:03.920
No, really: Oyama was a very real person whose
strength in Karate and tendency to fight bulls

00:01:03.920 --> 00:01:05.960
earned him the nickname "Godhand."

00:01:05.960 --> 00:01:09.930
Chiba was actually one of his students, and
starred in an entire trilogy of movies that

00:01:09.930 --> 00:01:12.549
were very loosely based on Oyama's life.

00:01:12.549 --> 00:01:17.280
Karate Bearfighter is the second, sandwiched
between Karate Bullfighter and Karate For

00:01:17.280 --> 00:01:18.409
Life.

00:01:18.409 --> 00:01:19.500
Pottersville

00:01:19.500 --> 00:01:23.890
To say that Pottersville is a bad movie is
sort of like saying outer space is cold.

00:01:23.890 --> 00:01:28.210
It is, but it’s far more accurate to talk
about it in terms of its boundless mysteries

00:01:28.210 --> 00:01:31.240
and the improbable presence of so many stars.

00:01:31.240 --> 00:01:34.930
Based on the title and the otherwise irrelevant
Christmastime setting, Pottersville seems

00:01:34.930 --> 00:01:39.140
like a weird sort of sequel to It's a Wonderful
Life, except that it takes place in the bad

00:01:39.140 --> 00:01:41.549
timeline where George Bailey was never born.

00:01:41.549 --> 00:01:45.079
There's even a scene at the end that mirrors
the heartwarming ending of the original…

00:01:45.079 --> 00:01:49.110
“To my big brother George, the richest man
in town.”

00:01:49.110 --> 00:01:53.180
...except that the version in Pottersville
involves one of the characters calling a town

00:01:53.180 --> 00:01:56.670
meeting to browbeat everyone into not sucking
as humans.

00:01:56.670 --> 00:02:00.869
Even that comes after 80 minutes of a plot
based around a man getting blackout drunk

00:02:00.869 --> 00:02:04.670
dressed up as a sasquatch because he finds
out his wife is a furry who’s been cheating

00:02:04.670 --> 00:02:06.190
on him with the local sheriff.

00:02:06.190 --> 00:02:08.870
The weirdest thing about it is how much star
power it has.

00:02:08.870 --> 00:02:13.370
Academy Award-winner Michael Shannon stars
with Christina Hendricks, Thomas Lennon, Ian

00:02:13.370 --> 00:02:15.020
McShane, and Ron Perlman.

00:02:15.020 --> 00:02:18.700
Since it definitely seems like they're getting
their scenes done in as few takes as humanly

00:02:18.700 --> 00:02:22.120
possible, it's hard to shake the feeling that
someone was actually trying to pull off the

00:02:22.120 --> 00:02:23.620
scheme from The Producers.

00:02:23.620 --> 00:02:28.760
“Under the right circumstances, a producer
could make more money with a flop than he

00:02:28.760 --> 00:02:30.620
could with a hit.”

00:02:30.870 --> 00:02:31.860
Rubber

00:02:31.870 --> 00:02:36.060
Despite the title, 2010's Rubber is not a
horror film about an evil prophylactic, although

00:02:36.060 --> 00:02:40.239
if that’s what you’re into, you may want
to check out 1996’s Killer Condom.

00:02:40.239 --> 00:02:44.410
Instead, this one’s an 82-minute French
movie about a tire named Robert that comes

00:02:44.410 --> 00:02:48.710
to life, gains psychokinetic powers, and then
goes on a killing spree.

00:02:48.710 --> 00:02:50.890
Because really, what else is a tire going
to do?

00:02:50.890 --> 00:02:55.790
It’s a weird idea but, Rubber seems to draw
its inspiration from some pretty weird places.

00:02:55.790 --> 00:02:59.820
At one point, there's a scene where a sheriff
tries to blow Robert up by dressing up a mannequin

00:02:59.820 --> 00:03:03.560
and then loading it up with dynamite, in the
style of Wile E. Coyote.

00:03:03.560 --> 00:03:07.690
According to director Quentin Dupieux, though,
his biggest inspiration was Pixar’s Wall-E.

00:03:07.920 --> 00:03:08.920
Sure.

00:03:09.200 --> 00:03:10.200
Why not?

00:03:10.920 --> 00:03:12.940
The 5,000 Fingers of Dr. T

00:03:12.950 --> 00:03:17.190
TV specials like How the Grinch Stole Christmas!
are among the most well-known and beloved

00:03:17.190 --> 00:03:21.460
television programs of all time, but here's
one piece of the Dr. Seuss canon that's rarely

00:03:21.460 --> 00:03:29.300
discussed: The 5,000 Fingers of Dr. T, a mind-boggling
look from 1953 at the horrors of piano lessons.

00:03:29.300 --> 00:03:33.120
Of course, it might be so obscure because
it was such a dismal failure that Seuss made

00:03:33.120 --> 00:03:34.670
no mention of it in his autobiography.

00:03:34.670 --> 00:03:38.959
There was even a moment on set when one child
actor got sick and led to a chain reaction

00:03:38.959 --> 00:03:44.340
of 150 boys all vomiting at once, which Seuss
compared to the film's critical reception.

00:03:44.340 --> 00:03:46.819
So of course, it’s absolutely worth watching.

00:03:46.819 --> 00:03:50.400
Set in a fantasy world that recreates Dr.
Seuss’s signature look on a live-action

00:03:50.400 --> 00:03:54.830
sound stage, the story follows 10-year-old
Billy as he's menaced by a nightmare version

00:03:54.830 --> 00:03:57.129
of his piano teacher, Dr. Terwilliker.

00:03:57.129 --> 00:04:01.760
In the dream world, Terwilliker's strict music
lessons are twisted into a mad plot to enslave

00:04:01.760 --> 00:04:06.700
500 children to play a single gigantic piano,
which will somehow help him brainwash and

00:04:06.700 --> 00:04:08.290
marry Billy’s mom.

00:04:08.290 --> 00:04:13.470
It's a truly strange film and the dizzying
set designs are as disorienting as you'd expect,

00:04:13.470 --> 00:04:18.470
but after a recent home video release, Dr.
T has managed to become a cult classic.

00:04:18.470 --> 00:04:19.470
Going Bananas

00:04:19.470 --> 00:04:24.160
The Cannon Group could fill an entire list
of unbelievable movies all by themselves,

00:04:24.160 --> 00:04:29.590
thanks to flicks like Ninja III: The Domination,
Hard Rock Zombies, and American Cyborg: Steel

00:04:29.590 --> 00:04:30.590
Warrior.

00:04:30.590 --> 00:04:34.250
Even compared to those, though, Going Bananas
is one of the weirdest movies of all time.

00:04:34.250 --> 00:04:36.070
In terms of plot, it's actually pretty basic.

00:04:36.070 --> 00:04:41.160
A shockingly intelligent orangutan named Bonzo
befriends a young American boy named Ben during

00:04:41.160 --> 00:04:45.950
a safari in Africa, which is referred to as
"the darkest and cruelest" of all continents

00:04:45.950 --> 00:04:48.940
because movies were still pretty racist in
1987.

00:04:48.940 --> 00:04:53.800
Hijinks ensue, including Bonzo being kidnapped
by a circus, flying an airplane, and straight

00:04:53.800 --> 00:04:54.950
up speaking English.

00:04:54.950 --> 00:05:00.040
And yes, in case you were, Bonzo's screeching,
falsetto voice is among the worst pieces of

00:05:00.040 --> 00:05:01.600
audio ever committed to film.

00:05:04.020 --> 00:05:07.480
“Bonzo love Ben."

00:05:08.860 --> 00:05:12.740
According to a documentary about Cannon Films,
the original plan was for the film to feature

00:05:12.750 --> 00:05:15.910
Clyde, the orangutan co-star of Every Which
Way But Loose.

00:05:15.910 --> 00:05:21.060
Execs began pitching the movie to Clyde, who,
and we cannot stress this enough, was an orangutan.

00:05:21.060 --> 00:05:24.640
"They see you and he looked at the monkey.

00:05:24.640 --> 00:05:28.140
And from now on he kept talking to the
monkey.”

00:05:28.480 --> 00:05:32.720
In the end, Clyde dropped out, probably because
the role would've required him to speak English

00:05:32.720 --> 00:05:37.350
and fly an airplane, and the part went to
Dee p Roy and a truly terrible ape costume

00:05:37.350 --> 00:05:38.390
instead.

00:05:38.390 --> 00:05:39.390
Thanks for watching!

